  "market_context": "NAS100 was in a clear downtrend within a descending parallel channel. Price made successive lower lows, but RSI formed a higher low—bullish regular divergence—while remaining inside the 70/30 RSI range. The setup occurred during the NY session with rising momentum.",
  "key_observation": "Price made a lower low while RSI made a higher low (bullish regular divergence) inside the 70/30 RSI range, confirmed by a strong rejection wick candle at the lower channel boundary.",
  "analysis_steps": [
    "Step 1: Identified a descending parallel channel on the 15-minute NAS100 chart with price making lower lows.",
    "Step 2: Observed that RSI formed a higher low between two price lower lows, confirming a bullish regular divergence.",
    "Step 3: Noted the divergence occurred while RSI remained within the 70/30 range, satisfying the 'secret sauce' entry condition.",
    "Step 4: Pinpointed a large rejection wick candle at the lower channel boundary as the entry trigger, using half the wick for stop placement.",
    "Step 5: Placed stop loss at the channel line (50 points), set initial 1:2 risk-to-reward, then trailed stop up through prior structure levels to achieve 1:3 R:R."
  ],
  "outcome": "Trade achieved 1:3 risk-to-reward by trailing stop through successive structure levels after hitting initial 1:2 target.",
  "lessons": "Bullish divergences inside the 70/30 RSI range within defined channels offer high-probability reversal entries; combining with rejection wicks and trailing stops maximizes reward while managing risk.",
